Stored credential test: isolate ambient proxies, retain certificate trust
=======================================================================

A credential test sends a stored secret to a caller-selected private device.
Target validation and DNS pinning must not be undermined by an unrelated
process-level HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY or ALL_PROXY setting. Previously HTTPX's
default environment handling could send the request to that proxy, although
the selected device address itself had passed validation. In explicitly enabled
plaintext fallback, the proxy could receive the synthetic Basic Authentication
header instead of the intended target. HTTPS CONNECT alone does not expose
that header; tests distinguish the two. This requires deployment environment
configuration and is not claimed as an unauthenticated remote exploit.

The endpoint constructs HTTPX's existing SSLContext explicitly, retaining the
same SSL_CERT_FILE, SSL_CERT_DIR and default certifi trust-store selection. The
client then uses trust_env=false, so process-level proxy variables cannot route
this stored-secret check. Certificate verification, hostname checking and the
existing explicit verify_ssl=false override retain their behavior. The code
uses HTTPX's exported create_ssl_context function, not private transport APIs.

Requests remain direct to the validated, pinned private target. Operators who
previously relied on ambient proxies for this endpoint must provide direct
reachability; this change does not add a supported proxy configuration. Other
HTTP clients, adapter overlay routing and discovery probes are unchanged.

The HTTP outcome remains a connectivity observation, not verified credential
authentication. Redirects are not followed; a completed HTTPS response never
causes a plaintext retry. Transport fallback still requires the existing
explicit allow_plaintext_http flag, and remains unsafe on untrusted networks.

Qualification covers all four uppercase/lowercase HTTPS/ALL proxy spellings,
HTTP proxy fallback, unaffected HTTPS requests, NO_PROXY controls, trusted CA
files/directories, untrusted certificates, absent private trust, explicit TLS
overrides and unchanged authorization/target denials. PostgreSQL rows, outgoing
TCP HTTP/TLS and recording proxy peers are real; credentials and authenticated
principals are synthetic and listeners are fixture-owned. No HTTPX, DNS or
security validator is mocked in the new regression/probe cases. Every API
case inspects all 51 existing credential rows. Separate incoming TCP HTTP
qualification complements ASGI regression requests. Shipping checks have their
own source-bound gates; local results do not imply completion of those gates.

Existing HTTP-outcome and TLS-default test doubles now inspect the SSLContext's
verification and hostname policy and assert proxy isolation. Their previous
outcome/TLS assertions are preserved; they do not stand in for real TLS tests.

No frontend or OpenAPI contract changes, historical-data rewrites, vendor
hardware operations, real login qualification or authority-revocation guarantee
are included. Response-size/total-time bounds and hostname/IPv6 transport
handling remain separate audit work. This is a focused hardening correction,
not an enterprise-readiness certification.
